IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cfi-dates`

-- Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) -- the questions of a family member who has lost someone.
--
-- SEAT: a member of the deceased person's family who sustained damage because of the death: the person for whose
-- benefit the action is brought (s 8(2)), and who may bring it in his or her own name if the personal representative
-- has not commenced it within 6 months (s 13). "Does this Act cover the death?", "Am I a member of the family?", "Is
-- the wrongdoer liable?", "Who brings the claim, and when can I bring it myself?", "What can the damages include?",
-- "Is my damages award reduced by the insurance or pension I received?", "How much of it do I get?", "Can I get the money
-- paid into court?", "My name was left off the claim: can I still share in it?". The defendant gets no seat here (see
-- NOTES.md); the court and the jury get none.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one timed step (the personal representative's 6 months to commence the action, after which a member of the
-- family may commence it, s 13(1)) is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole operative Act: s 2(2) (deaths before commencement), s 4 (definitions: child, parent,
-- personal representative, member of the family, the deeming rules of s 4(3)), s 5 (application, including the motor
-- accidents carve-out), s 6 (binds the Crown, in the s 7 question's description), s 7 (liability), s 8 (one action, by the
-- personal representative, for the family), s 10 (damages: heads, no-deduction list, the deceased's own settlement),
-- s 11 (contributory negligence), s 12 (payment into court), s 13 (action by a member of the family), s 14 (endorsement of
-- the writ, in answers), s 15 (omitted names; separate representation), s 16 (jury, in answers). Out: s 1 (short title),
-- s 2(1) (repeal), s 15(3) and (4) (procedure orders; powers not limited) -- machinery.
--
-- Edition: Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT), reprint REPC010 as in force at 28 November 2022 (the current
-- reprint on 6 November 2026), deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/Compensation-Fatal-Injuries-Act-1974-NT-current-reprint-REPC010.txt.
-- No regulation sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned by the Northern Territory. Quoted from an unofficial copy under
-- the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court or jury are INPUTS ("if the court
-- finds ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the
-- text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text does not answer is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT): the questions of a family member`

-- The endnotes' list of legislation: the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Ordinance 1974 (Act No. 36, 1974) commenced on
-- 23 September 1974. s 5(1) and s 2(2) speak of "the commencement of this Act".
`the commencement of this Act` MEANS `the calendar date` 23 9 1974

§§ `s 5 -- Does this Act cover the death`

-- s 5(1): "this Act applies only where a death occurs on or after the commencement of this Act, whether the act, neglect
-- or default that caused the death occurred before or after that commencement." A condition closed by "only": before
-- commencement the answer is the text's no, and s 2(2) says the 1938 Ordinance as amended continues to apply.
-- s 5(2): "This Act does not apply to or in relation to a death occurring in or as a result of an accident within the
-- meaning of the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979, except in those circumstances in which an action in respect of
-- that death is not precluded by that Act." A flat negative with an "except": whether the death arose from such an
-- accident, and whether that Act precludes an action, are borrowed from that Act and asked as facts (fork F1).
DECLARE `Does this Act cover the death` IS ONE OF
    `yes: this Act applies to the death (s 5)`
    `no: the death occurred before 23 September 1974; this Act applies only to later deaths, and the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Ordinance 1938 as amended continues to apply (s 5(1), s 2(2), the text's no)`
    `no: the death occurred in or as a result of a motor accident, and the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 precludes an action in respect of it (s 5(2), the text's no)`

@export Does the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) cover the death of my family member (s 5)? It covers deaths on or after 23 September 1974, but not a death in or from a motor accident unless the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 leaves an action open.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The day your family member died
      `a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in or as a result of an accident within the meaning of the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 (NT) (broadly, an accident involving a motor vehicle in the Territory)
      `an action is not precluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was such an accident: whether the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 still allows an action for damages in respect of the death (answer yes if you do not know that it is barred; it is ignored if there was no motor accident)
GIVETH A `Does this Act cover the death`
`s 5 -- does this Act cover the death` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the commencement of this Act` `is on or before` `the date of death`)
    THEN `no: the death occurred before 23 September 1974; this Act applies only to later deaths, and the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Ordinance 1938 as amended continues to apply (s 5(1), s 2(2), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `a motor accident` AND (NOT `an action is not precluded`)
         THEN `no: the death occurred in or as a result of a motor accident, and the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 precludes an action in respect of it (s 5(2), the text's no)`
         ELSE `yes: this Act applies to the death (s 5)`

GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE, `a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN, `an action is not precluded` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this Act covers the death` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded` IF
    `s 5 -- does this Act cover the death` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded`
        EQUALS `yes: this Act applies to the death (s 5)`

§§ `s 4 -- Am I a member of the deceased person's family`

-- s 4(1): "child ... includes an adopted child and a grand-child and a stepchild"; "parent ... includes an adoptive
-- parent, and a step-father, a step-mother, a grand-father, and a grand-mother".
-- s 4(2): "Each of the following persons is, for this Act, a member of a deceased person's family: (a) a spouse or de
-- facto partner ...; (b) a child ...; (c) a person to whom the deceased person stood, immediately before his or her
-- death, in loco parentis; (d) a person who stood, immediately before his or her death, in loco parentis to the deceased
-- person; (e) a parent ...; (f) a brother, a sister, a half-brother and a half-sister ...; (g) a former spouse or de facto
-- partner".
-- s 4(3)(b): a child born alive after the death is treated as born before it; (d): an infant to whom the deceased stood
-- in loco parentis is treated as the deceased's child.
-- The s 4(2) list is not closed with "only", and "child" and "parent" are defined by "includes": for anyone else the
-- answer is NOTHING-kind (the rulings of 2026-11-04), not the text's no (fork F2). "spouse", "de facto partner" and
-- "in loco parentis" are borrowed terms, asked through the choice.
DECLARE `My relationship to the deceased` IS ONE OF
    `spouse`
    `de facto partner`
    `former spouse`
    `former de facto partner`
    `child, including an adopted child`
    `grandchild`
    `stepchild`
    `child born alive after the death`
    `a person to whom the deceased stood in loco parentis immediately before the death`
    `a person who stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death`
    `parent, including an adoptive parent`
    `step-father or step-mother`
    `grandfather or grandmother`
    `brother or sister`
    `half-brother or half-sister`
    `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, cousin, niece, nephew, step-brother, step-sister, fiance or friend)`

DECLARE `Am I a member of the family` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a spouse or de facto partner (s 4(2)(a))`
    `yes: a child, which includes an adopted child, a grandchild and a stepchild (s 4(1), s 4(2)(b))`
    `yes: a child born alive after the death, treated as born before it (s 4(3)(b), s 4(2)(b))`
    `yes: a person to whom the deceased stood in loco parentis immediately before the death (s 4(2)(c); an infant is also treated as a child, s 4(3)(d))`
    `yes: a person who stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death (s 4(2)(d))`
    `yes: a parent, which includes an adoptive parent, a step-parent and a grandparent (s 4(1), s 4(2)(e))`
    `yes: a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister (s 4(2)(f))`
    `yes: a former spouse or de facto partner (s 4(2)(g))`
    `the Act does not name this relationship in s 4(2) or in its definitions of child and parent; it does not say whether you are a member of the family (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Am I a member of the deceased person's family under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) (s 4)? Only members of the family can benefit from a claim for a wrongful death: spouses and de facto partners (current and former), children (including adopted, step- and grandchildren), parents (including adoptive, step- and grandparents), brothers and sisters (including half), and people in a parent-child relationship (in loco parentis) with the deceased.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died (immediately before the death, for a relationship in loco parentis)
GIVETH A `Am I a member of the family`
`s 4 -- am I a member of the family` `my relationship` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my relationship`
    WHEN `spouse` THEN `yes: a spouse or de facto partner (s 4(2)(a))`
    WHEN `de facto partner` THEN `yes: a spouse or de facto partner (s 4(2)(a))`
    WHEN `former spouse` THEN `yes: a former spouse or de facto partner (s 4(2)(g))`
    WHEN `former de facto partner` THEN `yes: a former spouse or de facto partner (s 4(2)(g))`
    WHEN `child, including an adopted child` THEN `yes: a child, which includes an adopted child, a grandchild and a stepchild (s 4(1), s 4(2)(b))`
    WHEN `grandchild` THEN `yes: a child, which includes an adopted child, a grandchild and a stepchild (s 4(1), s 4(2)(b))`
    WHEN `stepchild` THEN `yes: a child, which includes an adopted child, a grandchild and a stepchild (s 4(1), s 4(2)(b))`
    WHEN `child born alive after the death` THEN `yes: a child born alive after the death, treated as born before it (s 4(3)(b), s 4(2)(b))`
    WHEN `a person to whom the deceased stood in loco parentis immediately before the death` THEN `yes: a person to whom the deceased stood in loco parentis immediately before the death (s 4(2)(c); an infant is also treated as a child, s 4(3)(d))`
    WHEN `a person who stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death` THEN `yes: a person who stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death (s 4(2)(d))`
    WHEN `parent, including an adoptive parent` THEN `yes: a parent, which includes an adoptive parent, a step-parent and a grandparent (s 4(1), s 4(2)(e))`
    WHEN `step-father or step-mother` THEN `yes: a parent, which includes an adoptive parent, a step-parent and a grandparent (s 4(1), s 4(2)(e))`
    WHEN `grandfather or grandmother` THEN `yes: a parent, which includes an adoptive parent, a step-parent and a grandparent (s 4(1), s 4(2)(e))`
    WHEN `brother or sister` THEN `yes: a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister (s 4(2)(f))`
    WHEN `half-brother or half-sister` THEN `yes: a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister (s 4(2)(f))`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not name this relationship in s 4(2) or in its definitions of child and parent; it does not say whether you are a member of the family (NOTHING-kind)`

GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am named as a member of the family` `my relationship` IF
    NOT (`s 4 -- am I a member of the family` `my relationship`
            EQUALS `the Act does not name this relationship in s 4(2) or in its definitions of child and parent; it does not say whether you are a member of the family (NOTHING-kind)`)

§§ `s 7 -- Is the wrongdoer liable to an action for the death`

-- s 7(1): "Where the death of a person is caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default and the act, neglect or default is
-- such that it would, if death had not ensued, have entitled the person injured to maintain an action and recover
-- damages in respect of the injury, the person who would have been liable ... is liable to an action for damages despite
-- the death ... and irrespective of whether the death ... was caused by circumstances that amount in law to an offence."
-- s 7(2): the deceased's own settlement, release or judgment does not, subject to s 10(5), affect the liability.
-- s 6: "This Act binds the Crown." Both elements of s 7(1) are findings for the court: asked as facts. When either fails,
-- the "where" fails: the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05).
DECLARE `Is the wrongdoer liable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the person who would have been liable to the deceased is liable to an action for damages for the death, even if the death also amounts to an offence (s 7(1))`
    `yes: the person who would have been liable to the deceased is liable to an action for damages for the death; the deceased's own settlement, release or judgment does not affect that liability, but a payment under it made within 6 months after the wrongful act is taken into account in the damages (s 7(2), s 10(5))`
    `no, not under s 7: the death was not caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default for which the deceased could have sued had he or she lived (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `not under this Act: it does not cover the death (s 5, the text's no)`

@export Is the person who caused my family member's death liable to a claim for damages under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) (s 7)? They are liable where the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default that would have let the deceased sue and recover damages had he or she lived, even if it was also a crime. The Act binds the Crown.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The day your family member died
      `a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in or as a result of an accident within the meaning of the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 (NT)
      `an action is not precluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was such an accident: whether that Act still allows an action for damages in respect of the death (ignored otherwise)
      `caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default of the other person (for example negligence), as a court would find
      `the deceased could have sued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, had your family member lived, he or she could have sued that person and recovered damages for the injury (for example, no defence would have defeated the claim)
      `the deceased settled or sued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your family member, before dying, settled the claim, released the wrongdoer, or obtained a judgment for the injury
GIVETH A `Is the wrongdoer liable`
`s 7 -- is the wrongdoer liable` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded` `caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` `the deceased could have sued` `the deceased settled or sued` MEANS
    IF NOT (`this Act covers the death` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded`)
    THEN `not under this Act: it does not cover the death (s 5, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` AND `the deceased could have sued`)
         THEN `no, not under s 7: the death was not caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default for which the deceased could have sued had he or she lived (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
         ELSE IF `the deceased settled or sued`
              THEN `yes: the person who would have been liable to the deceased is liable to an action for damages for the death; the deceased's own settlement, release or judgment does not affect that liability, but a payment under it made within 6 months after the wrongful act is taken into account in the damages (s 7(2), s 10(5))`
              ELSE `yes: the person who would have been liable to the deceased is liable to an action for damages for the death, even if the death also amounts to an offence (s 7(1))`

§§ `s 8 and s 13 -- Who brings the action, and can I bring it myself`

-- s 8(1): "Not more than one action may be brought against any one person in respect of a death." A flat negative.
-- s 8(2): "Subject to section 13, any such action must be brought by and in the name of the personal representative of
-- the deceased person for the benefit of those members of the deceased person's family who sustained damage because of
-- his or her death."
-- s 13(1): "Where an action has not been commenced by and in the name of the personal representative of a deceased person
-- within 6 months after the death of the deceased person, any one or more of the persons for whose benefit an action may
-- be brought may bring such an action." s 13(2): it is for the benefit of the same persons, on the same provisions.
-- s 14: the writ must name each person for whose benefit the action is brought and that person's relationship to the
-- deceased.
--
-- The timed step is a regulative rule: the personal representative has 6 months after the death to commence the action
-- (DO: neither required nor forbidden, but if it is not done in time the family's permission follows); after that, a
-- member of the family MAY commence it. The 6 months are counted to the same day of the month (cfi-dates.l4, fork F3).
DECLARE `Who acts` IS ONE OF `the personal representative`, `a member of the family who sustained damage`
DECLARE `Step in the action` IS ONE OF `commence the action`

GIVEN `the days in the 6 months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Who acts` `Step in the action`
`s 13 -- the personal representative's 6 months, then the family` `the days in the 6 months` MEANS
    PARTY `the personal representative`
    DO `commence the action`
    WITHIN `the days in the 6 months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST
        PARTY `a member of the family who sustained damage`
        MAY `commence the action`

-- Runs the rule: may a member of the family commence the action on this day (counted in days from the death), the
-- personal representative not having commenced it? FULFILLED means yes; a rule still waiting for the personal
-- representative means not yet. It takes no DEONTIC from outside and is not exported.
GIVEN `the days in the 6 months` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 13 -- a member of the family may commence on this day` `the days in the 6 months` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 13 -- the personal representative's 6 months, then the family` `the days in the 6 months`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `a member of the family who sustained damage` `commence the action` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Who brings the action` IS ONE OF
    `you may bring the action yourself, in your own name, for the benefit of every member of the family who sustained damage because of the death; the writ must name each of them and their relationship to the deceased (s 13, s 14)`
    `not yet: until 6 months after the death only the personal representative may bring the action, for your benefit and the benefit of the other members of the family who sustained damage (s 8(2), s 13(1))`
    `no new action: not more than one action may be brought against any one person in respect of a death (s 8(1), the text's no); the action already brought is for the benefit of every member of the family who sustained damage, and if your name was left out, see s 15`
    `the action is not for your benefit: it is for the members of the family who sustained damage because of the death (s 8(2), the text's no)`
    `the Act does not name your relationship among the members of the family; it does not say whether the action is for your benefit (NOTHING-kind)`
    `not under this Act: it does not cover the death, so no action lies under it (s 5, the text's no)`

@export Who brings the claim for my family member's death, and can I bring it myself (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) ss 8, 13)? Only one action lies against each wrongdoer. The deceased's personal representative (executor or administrator) brings it for the family; if they have not done so within 6 months after the death, any family member who suffered loss may bring it for all of them.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The day your family member died
      `a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in or as a result of an accident within the meaning of the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 (NT)
      `an action is not precluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was such an accident: whether that Act still allows an action for damages in respect of the death (ignored otherwise)
      `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died
      `I sustained damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage because of the death (for example, you lost financial support or services, paid funeral costs, or suffered grief)
      `an action already brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action under this Act in respect of the death has already been commenced against this wrongdoer, by the personal representative or by a member of the family
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (or the day you mean to commence the action)
GIVETH A `Who brings the action`
`s 8 and s 13 -- who brings the action, and can I bring it myself` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded` `my relationship` `I sustained damage` `an action already brought` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT (`this Act covers the death` `the date of death` `a motor accident` `an action is not precluded`)
    THEN `not under this Act: it does not cover the death, so no action lies under it (s 5, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`I am named as a member of the family` `my relationship`)
         THEN `the Act does not name your relationship among the members of the family; it does not say whether the action is for your benefit (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF NOT `I sustained damage`
              THEN `the action is not for your benefit: it is for the members of the family who sustained damage because of the death (s 8(2), the text's no)`
              ELSE IF `an action already brought`
                   THEN `no new action: not more than one action may be brought against any one person in respect of a death (s 8(1), the text's no); the action already brought is for the benefit of every member of the family who sustained damage, and if your name was left out, see s 15`
                   ELSE IF `s 13 -- a member of the family may commence on this day` `the days in the 6 months` `the day counted`
                        THEN `you may bring the action yourself, in your own name, for the benefit of every member of the family who sustained damage because of the death; the writ must name each of them and their relationship to the deceased (s 13, s 14)`
                        ELSE `not yet: until 6 months after the death only the personal representative may bring the action, for your benefit and the benefit of the other members of the family who sustained damage (s 8(2), s 13(1))`
    WHERE
        `the days in the 6 months` MEANS `the days from` `the date of death` `to` (`the last day of 6 months after` `the date of death`)
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `the date of death` `to` `today`
        `the day counted` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

@export From what day may I, as a member of the family, bring the claim for the death myself if the personal representative has not (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) s 13(1))? The personal representative has 6 months after the death; the answer is the first day after those 6 months.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The day your family member died
GIVETH A DATE
`s 13 -- the first day I may bring the action myself` `the date of death` MEANS
    (`the last day of 6 months after` `the date of death`) `plus days` 1

§§ `s 10 -- What the damages can include`

-- s 10(1): the court (or the jury, s 16) "may award to the parties respectively for whose benefit the action is brought,
-- such damages as it may think proportioned to the injury resulting from the death". How much is a judgment: not encoded.
-- s 10(3): "Damages in an action may include: (a) the reasonable expenses of burial or disposal of the remains ...
-- incurred by a person for whose benefit the action is brought; (b) the reasonable medical and hospital expenses of the
-- deceased person in relation to the injury ... incurred by [such] a person ...; (c) if the deceased person is survived
-- by a spouse or de facto partner -- damages for loss or impairment of consortium ...; (d) if the deceased person
-- customarily performed household services -- the reasonable expenses ... of the hire of help to perform those
-- services; (e) if the deceased person is survived by a child of his or hers who is an infant: (i) the reasonable
-- expenses ... of the hire of a person to live in the home and care for the infant until the infant attains the age of
-- 18 years; and (ii) damages on account of loss of care and guidance of the child by the deceased person as a parent; and
-- (f) solatium."
-- "may include" opens the list: a head not named is not excluded (no answer is given for it). A head whose "if" fails
-- is simply not listed. Which expenses are "reasonable" is for the court. Solatium is not glossed in the Act: money for
-- grief and suffering.
DECLARE `Head of damages` IS ONE OF
    `damages the court thinks proportioned to the injury resulting from the death, such as lost financial support (s 10(1))`
    `the reasonable expenses of burial or disposal of the remains (s 10(3)(a))`
    `the reasonable medical and hospital expenses of the deceased for the fatal injury (s 10(3)(b))`
    `damages for loss or impairment of consortium, for the surviving spouse or de facto partner (s 10(3)(c))`
    `the reasonable expenses of hiring help to perform the household services the deceased customarily performed (s 10(3)(d))`
    `the reasonable expenses of hiring a person to live in the home and care for the infant child until 18 (s 10(3)(e)(i))`
    `damages for the infant child's loss of the deceased's care and guidance as a parent (s 10(3)(e)(ii))`
    `solatium: money for grief and suffering (s 10(3)(f))`

@export What can the damages for my family member's death include (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) s 10(1) and (3))? Besides damages for the loss caused by the death and solatium, they may include burial and medical costs paid by a family member, loss of consortium for a surviving partner, the cost of replacing household services, and, for an infant child, a live-in carer until 18 and loss of a parent's care and guidance.
GIVEN `burial expenses paid by a family member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or another member of the family for whose benefit the action is brought incurred expenses for the burial or other disposal of the remains (for example cremation)
      `medical expenses paid by a family member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or another member of the family for whose benefit the action is brought incurred medical or hospital expenses of the deceased for the injury that caused the death
      `survived by a spouse or de facto partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased left a surviving spouse or de facto partner (a former spouse or partner does not count here)
      `customarily performed household services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased customarily performed household services (for example cooking, cleaning, maintenance, child care)
      `survived by an infant child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased left a child of his or her own who was under 18 at the death (including one born alive after the death)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Head of damages`
`s 10 -- what can the damages include` `burial expenses paid by a family member` `medical expenses paid by a family member` `survived by a spouse or de facto partner` `customarily performed household services` `survived by an infant child` MEANS
    concat (LIST (LIST `damages the court thinks proportioned to the injury resulting from the death, such as lost financial support (s 10(1))`)
                 , (`only if` `burial expenses paid by a family member` `the reasonable expenses of burial or disposal of the remains (s 10(3)(a))`)
                 , (`only if` `medical expenses paid by a family member` `the reasonable medical and hospital expenses of the deceased for the fatal injury (s 10(3)(b))`)
                 , (`only if` `survived by a spouse or de facto partner` `damages for loss or impairment of consortium, for the surviving spouse or de facto partner (s 10(3)(c))`)
                 , (`only if` `customarily performed household services` `the reasonable expenses of hiring help to perform the household services the deceased customarily performed (s 10(3)(d))`)
                 , (`only if` `survived by an infant child` `the reasonable expenses of hiring a person to live in the home and care for the infant child until 18 (s 10(3)(e)(i))`)
                 , (`only if` `survived by an infant child` `damages for the infant child's loss of the deceased's care and guidance as a parent (s 10(3)(e)(ii))`)
                 , (LIST `solatium: money for grief and suffering (s 10(3)(f))`))

GIVEN `the condition` IS A BOOLEAN, `the head` IS A `Head of damages`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Head of damages`
`only if` `the condition` `the head` MEANS
    IF `the condition` THEN LIST `the head` ELSE EMPTY

-- s 10(3)(e): "if the deceased person is survived by a child of his or her who is an infant", and (i) "until the infant
-- attains the age of 18 years". "infant" is not defined in the Act: read as a person under 18, the age (i) uses (fork F4).
-- A child born alive after the death is treated as born before it (s 4(3)(b)), and so as an infant at the death.
DECLARE `How long the carer expenses run` IS ONE OF
    `until the day the child attains 18 (s 10(3)(e)(i))` HAS `that day` IS A DATE
    `not under s 10(3)(e): the child was not an infant when the deceased died (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Until what day can the cost of a live-in carer for my child be claimed after a parent's death (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) s 10(3)(e)(i))? If the child was under 18 when the parent died (or was born after the death), the damages may include the reasonable cost of hiring a person to live in the home and care for the child until the child turns 18.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the child's parent (the deceased) died
      `the child's date of birth` IS A DATE @desc The child's date of birth (it may be after the death, for a child born after it)
GIVETH A `How long the carer expenses run`
`s 10(3)(e) -- until when can carer expenses for my child be claimed` `the date of death` `the child's date of birth` MEANS
    IF (`the days from` `the date of death` `to` `the eighteenth birthday`) GREATER THAN 0
    THEN `until the day the child attains 18 (s 10(3)(e)(i))` `the eighteenth birthday`
    ELSE `not under s 10(3)(e): the child was not an infant when the deceased died (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    WHERE
        `the eighteenth birthday` MEANS `the day of the 18th birthday of` `the child's date of birth`

§§ `s 10(4) and (5) -- Is my damages award reduced by money I received because of the death`

-- s 10(4): "In assessing damages in action, no reduction must be made on account of: (a) a sum paid or payable on the
-- death of, or personal injury to, the deceased person under a contract of insurance; (b) a sum paid or payable out of a
-- superannuation, provident or like fund, or by way of benefit from a friendly society, benefit society, lodge or trade
-- union; (c) a sum paid or payable by any government or person consequent upon the death or injury ... being: (i) a
-- payment instead of furlough or long service leave; or (ii) a payment by way of pension, social service benefit or
-- repatriation benefit; (d) a sum paid or payable as a gratuity consequent upon the death ...; (e) any sum in respect of
-- the acquisition by a member of the deceased person's family, consequent upon the death, of, or an interest in, a
-- dwelling used at any time as the home of the member, or ... of the household contents ...; (f) a premium that would
-- have become payable under a contract of insurance in respect of the life of the deceased person if he or she had
-- lived ...; (g) any other gain or benefits that have accrued or may accrue to the deceased person's estate or to any
-- person for whose benefit an action is brought consequent upon the death ...; (h) the marriage or entry into a de facto
-- relationship, or the prospects of doing so, of a surviving spouse or de facto partner or a surviving former spouse or
-- de facto partner." A flat negative: the text's no to a reduction.
-- s 10(5): "A payment made as a result of a settlement, release or judgment in respect of the wrongful act ... made, given
-- or obtained by the deceased person within the period of 6 months after the commission or occurrence of the wrongful
-- act ... must be taken into account in assessing damages". For a settlement made later than that the Act says nothing
-- (s 7(2) keeps the liability; s 10(5) reaches only the first 6 months): NOTHING-kind (fork F5).
DECLARE `What I received` IS ONE OF
    `(a) a sum paid or payable under a contract of insurance on the death of, or personal injury to, the deceased`
    `(b) a sum from a superannuation, provident or like fund, or a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society, lodge or trade union`
    `(c)(i) a payment instead of furlough or long service leave, paid by a government or person because of the death or injury`
    `(c)(ii) a pension, social service benefit or repatriation benefit, paid by a government or person because of the death or injury`
    `(d) a gratuity paid or payable because of the death`
    `(e) a sum for acquiring, because of the death, a home I have lived in (or an interest in it) or its household contents`
    `(f) life insurance premiums on the deceased that will no longer have to be paid`
    `(g) any other gain or benefit to the estate or to a member of the family, because of the death (for example an inheritance)`
    `(h) the remarriage or new de facto relationship of the surviving spouse or partner (or former spouse or partner), or the prospects of it`
    `a payment under a settlement, release or judgment the deceased made, gave or obtained for the wrongful act before dying`

DECLARE `Is my award reduced` IS ONE OF
    `no: no reduction may be made on account of it in assessing the damages (s 10(4), the text's no)`
    `yes: it must be taken into account in assessing the damages, since the deceased's settlement, release or judgment was made within 6 months after the wrongful act (s 10(5))`
    `the Act does not say: s 10(5) reaches only a settlement, release or judgment made within 6 months after the wrongful act, and this one was made later (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Is the damages award for my family member's death reduced because of insurance, superannuation, a pension, an inheritance or other money received because of the death (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) s 10(4), (5))? None of those reduce it, nor do a partner's remarriage prospects; but a payment under the deceased's own settlement or judgment made within 6 months after the wrongful act is taken into account.
GIVEN `what I received` IS A `What I received` @desc What was received or is in prospect (pick the paragraph of s 10(4) that fits, or the deceased's own settlement)
      `the day of the wrongful act` IS A DATE @desc For the deceased's own settlement: the day the wrongful act, neglect or default was committed or occurred (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `the day of the settlement` IS A DATE @desc For the deceased's own settlement: the day the deceased made the settlement, gave the release or obtained the judgment (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Is my award reduced`
`s 10(4) and (5) -- is my award reduced by it` `what I received` `the day of the wrongful act` `the day of the settlement` MEANS
    IF `what I received` EQUALS `a payment under a settlement, release or judgment the deceased made, gave or obtained for the wrongful act before dying`
    THEN IF `the day of the settlement` `is on or before` (`the last day of 6 months after` `the day of the wrongful act`)
         THEN `yes: it must be taken into account in assessing the damages, since the deceased's settlement, release or judgment was made within 6 months after the wrongful act (s 10(5))`
         ELSE `the Act does not say: s 10(5) reaches only a settlement, release or judgment made within 6 months after the wrongful act, and this one was made later (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE `no: no reduction may be made on account of it in assessing the damages (s 10(4), the text's no)`

§§ `s 10(2) and s 11 -- How much of the damages I receive`

-- s 11(1): where the deceased died partly of his or her own wrong and partly of another's, "any damages recoverable in an
-- action under this Act must be reduced to the same extent as if they were damages in an action so brought for the
-- benefit of the estate of the deceased person" (under Part II of the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956;
-- "wrong" as in its Part V). The extent of that reduction is that Act's and the court's: asked as a percentage.
-- s 10(2): "The amount of damages recovered ... must, after deducting the costs not recovered from the defendant, be
-- divided amongst the persons for whose benefit the action is brought in such shares as the court determines." The share
-- is the court's (or jury's, s 16): asked as a percentage. If the unrecovered costs exceed the damages, nothing is left
-- to divide (fork F6).
@export How much of the damages for my family member's death will I receive (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) s 10(2), s 11)? The damages are first reduced for the deceased's own share of the blame (if any), then the legal costs not recovered from the defendant are deducted, and the rest is divided among the family members in the shares the court decides.
GIVEN `the damages assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The total damages assessed for all the family members, in dollars, before any reduction for the deceased's own fault
      `the reduction for the deceased's own fault` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage (0 to 100) by which damages in an action for the deceased's estate would be reduced because the deceased was partly to blame (0 if not at all)
      `the costs not recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs of the action not recovered from the defendant, in dollars (0 if none)
      `my share` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage (0 to 100) of the damages the court gives you as your share
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 10(2) and s 11 -- how much do I receive` `the damages assessed` `the reduction for the deceased's own fault` `the costs not recovered` `my share` MEANS
    (`what is left to divide` TIMES `my share`) DIVIDED BY 100
    WHERE
        `the damages recovered` MEANS (`the damages assessed` TIMES (100 MINUS `the reduction for the deceased's own fault`)) DIVIDED BY 100
        `what is left to divide` MEANS
            IF `the costs not recovered` AT LEAST `the damages recovered`
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the damages recovered` MINUS `the costs not recovered`

§§ `s 12 -- Money the defendant pays into court`

-- s 12(1): the defendant may pay money into court as compensation for the persons for whose benefit the action is
-- brought. s 12(2): "no portion of that amount must be paid out of court except under an order of the court." A flat
-- negative with an "except".
DECLARE `Can I be paid the money in court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court has ordered it, and money paid into court is paid out under the court's order (s 12(2))`
    `not without an order of the court: no part of the money paid into court may be paid out except under an order of the court (s 12(2), the text's no)`
    `s 12 does not arise: the defendant has not paid money into court (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export Can I be paid my share of money the defendant paid into court in the claim for my family member's death (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) s 12)? Money paid into court as compensation can be paid out only under an order of the court.
GIVEN `money paid into court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has paid money into court as compensation in the action
      `the court has ordered payment out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made an order for the money (or your share of it) to be paid out
GIVETH A `Can I be paid the money in court`
`s 12 -- can I be paid the money in court` `money paid into court` `the court has ordered payment out` MEANS
    IF NOT `money paid into court`
    THEN `s 12 does not arise: the defendant has not paid money into court (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF `the court has ordered payment out`
         THEN `yes: the court has ordered it, and money paid into court is paid out under the court's order (s 12(2))`
         ELSE `not without an order of the court: no part of the money paid into court may be paid out except under an order of the court (s 12(2), the text's no)`

§§ `s 14 and s 15 -- If my name was left off the action`

-- Situation: an action has been commenced, and the writ does not name you. s 14: the writ must name each person for
-- whose benefit the action is brought and that person's relationship to the deceased.
-- s 15(1): "Where: (a) an action has been commenced; and (b) the court is satisfied that a person whose name is not
-- included ... is a person whose name should have been so included; the court may, on application made by or on behalf of
-- that person, or of its own motion, order the action to proceed as if the name of that person had been so included."
-- The court's satisfaction is a judgment, asked as a fact; the order is a discretion ("may"), never decided.
-- s 15(2): the court may order that any of the persons be separately represented.
DECLARE `If my name was left off` IS ONE OF
    `your name is in the action: it is brought for your benefit (s 14); the court may also order that you be separately represented (s 15(2))`
    `the court may order the action to proceed as if your name had been included, on your application or of its own motion (s 15(1)); whether it does is the court's discretion; it may also order separate representation (s 15(2))`
    `s 15(1) does not arise: the court is not satisfied that your name should have been included (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 15 does not arise: no action has been commenced (the text's no: its "where" fails); see s 8 and s 13 on who may bring one`

@export My name was left off the claim for my family member's death: can I still share in it (Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NT) ss 14, 15)? If the court is satisfied your name should have been included, it may order the action to proceed as if it had been, on your application or of its own motion.
GIVEN `an action commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action under this Act for the death has been commenced
      `my name is included` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the writ or other originating process names you as a person for whose benefit the action is brought
      `the court is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is (or would be) satisfied that your name should have been included (for example, you are a member of the family who suffered loss by the death)
GIVETH A `If my name was left off`
`s 15 -- if my name was left off the action` `an action commenced` `my name is included` `the court is satisfied` MEANS
    IF NOT `an action commenced`
    THEN `s 15 does not arise: no action has been commenced (the text's no: its "where" fails); see s 8 and s 13 on who may bring one`
    ELSE IF `my name is included`
         THEN `your name is in the action: it is brought for your benefit (s 14); the court may also order that you be separately represented (s 15(2))`
         ELSE IF `the court is satisfied`
              THEN `the court may order the action to proceed as if your name had been included, on your application or of its own motion (s 15(1)); whether it does is the court's discretion; it may also order separate representation (s 15(2))`
              ELSE `s 15(1) does not arise: the court is not satisfied that your name should have been included (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
